    "id": "1.16",
    "number": "1.16",
    "section": "Adhyāya 1 — Arjuna-viṣāda (Grief)",
    "devanagari": "अनन्तविजयं राजा कुन्तीपुत्रो युधिष्ठिरः। नकुलः सहदेवश्च सुघोषमणिपुष्पकौ॥1.16॥",
    "iast": "anantavijayaṃ rājā kuntīputro yudhiṣṭhiraḥ nakulaḥ sahadevaśca sughoṣamaṇipuṣpakau",
    "conceptIds": ["gita-visada"],
    "interpretiveNotes": [{"note": "Kashmir Series of Texts and Studies No. LXIV (1943): Bhagavadgītā with Sarvatobhadra of Rājānaka Rāmakantha, ed. Madhusudan Kaul Shastri; Sanskrit-only source, English rendering original to this project."}, {"note": "Rāmakantha versification (PDF p.12, KSTS p.12, scan-verified p22.png): the edition's 1.16 reads pauṇḍraṃ dadhmau mahāśaṅkhaṃ bhīmakarmā vṛkodaraḥ / anantavijayaṃ rājā kuntīputro yudhiṣṭhiraḥ — i.e. our 1.15b + our 1.16a. Our 1.16b (nakulaḥ sahadevaś…) stands as its 1.17a. We keep vulgate versification for id-stability."}, {"note": "Sarvatobhadra (PDF pp.15–16, KSTS pp.15–16): verses 1.2–1.19 are nigada-vyākhyāta — self-evident, no word-gloss — their tātparya is karma-ghoratva-pratipādana: the terrible massed form of the action Arjuna will seek to abandon."}],
    "translation": "King Yudhiṣṭhira, Kuntī's son, blew Anantavijaya — and Nakula and Sahadeva blew Sughoṣa and Maṇipuṣpaka.",
    "commentary": "Plain meaning, fully drawn out: the dharma-house sounds, and the verse is built as steadfastness flanked by twins. Anantavijayaṃ rājā kuntīputro yudhiṣṭhiraḥ — 'King Yudhiṣṭhira, Kuntī's son, (blew) Unending-Victory': rājā (the king — the only rājan named on the Pāṇḍava side; Duryodhana called himself rājā at 1.2, here kingship is ascribed where it belongs), kuntīputraḥ (Kuntī's son — the eldest of the mother-line, Kunti the mater dolorosa whose suffering authorizes the war; named by mother, not father, for dharma here is maternal endurance rather than paternal claim), yudhiṣṭhiraḥ (firm in battle — yudhi + sthira: steadfast where fighting happens, the still point inside combat), anantavijayam (unending victory — an-anta, without end: the conch names the outcome in advance, triumph not as hope but as nature; what cannot end cannot properly be said to be won — it is confessed). Nakulaḥ sahadevaś ca — 'Nakula and Sahadeva': the twins (Mādrī's sons, the Aśvin-born beauties — symmetry incarnate, the war's youngest charioteers, last named because last born, yet sounding before the elders of 1.17 — youth given voice early, as at 1.6); ca binds them as a pair inside the verse's pair-structure. Sughoṣa-maṇipuṣpakau — '(blew) Sughoṣa and Jewel-Flower': sughosa (good-proclamation — su + ghoṣa: sound that announces rightly, speech that declares instead of braying), maṇipuṣpaka (jewel-flower — maṇi + puṣpa: blossom of gem, beauty that blooms hard and precious; the twin shells share one dual accusative, two voices one flowering). The verse's music is thus major-key after 1.15's wrath-key: Bhīma devoured, now Dharma proclaims and Beauty twins answer — ananta (endlessness) after bhīma-karman (terror-deeds): what terror consumes, steadfastness keeps. Sarvatobhadra meaning, fully drawn out: nigada-vyākhyāta — yet the three names are upadeśa in miniature, the śāstra's dharmi-traya (dharma, niyama, viveka) sounding. Yudhiṣṭhira figures dharma-sthiti (abiding in righteousness — sthira where yudh happens: the Self's immovability inside action, the exact posture 2.38–2.48 will teach as sthita-prajñā and niṣkāma-karma); his Anantavijaya is ātma-jaya proclaimed beforehand — the Ātman's victory (an-anta, endless because outside time) needing no staking (against 1.9's tyakta-jīvita on mad-arthe: delusion wagers life on the ego, dharma merely announces what cannot be lost). Nakula figures dama-saundarya (restraint-beauty — the younger twin, loveliness disciplined: senses restrained shine, hence Sughoṣa, right-proclamation — restrained speech proclaims rightly, where 1.13's gomukha bellowed). Sahadeva figures sahita-viveka (wisdom-with, companionship-discrimination — saha + deva: the divine with-us, counsel that accompanies rather than commands, hence Maṇipuṣpaka, jewel-flower — discrimination blooming precious and hard, śabda-brahman flowering as beauty when speech serves truth). Kuntīputra-maternal naming carries the doctrine: this triad is born of Kuntī (invocation-endurance, the mother who summoned gods by mantra and suffered for it) — dharma, restraint, and wisdom are mantra-born, not blood-seized; they arrive by calling (like Karṇa, like the Pāṇḍavas themselves) rather than by grabbing (like Jayadratha's parigraha, 1.8). So 1.16 sounds the Self's economy against 1.9's ego-economy: ananta (already-won) vs. tyakta-jīvita (wagered-away) — and prepares 1.19, where these very voices, with all the named shells, will break the other heart.",
    "keyPoints": ["rājā: sole true kingship ascribed, not seized", "kuntīputra: maternal naming — endurance authorizes war", "yudhiṣṭhira: still point inside combat", "anantavijaya: outcome confessed, not hoped", "twins last-born yet early-voiced, as at 1.6", "sughoṣa: restrained speech proclaims rightly", "maṇipuṣpaka: discrimination blooming jewel-hard", "major key after wrath key: terror then steadfastness", "mantra-born triad vs blood-seized powers", "ananta already-won vs tyakta-jīvita wagered-away"],
    "wordMeaning": "anantavijayam (accusative singular neuter, an-anta + vijaya) — Unending-Victory; rājā (nominative singular of rājan) — the king; kuntīputraḥ (nominative singular masculine, kuntī + putra) — Kuntī's son; yudhiṣṭhiraḥ (nominative singular masculine, yudhi + sthira) — Yudhiṣṭhira, firm in battle; nakulaḥ (nominative singular) — Nakula; sahadevaḥ (nominative singular) — Sahadeva; ca (conjunction) — and; sughoṣa-maṇipuṣpakau (accusative dual masculine dvandva: sughoṣa good-proclamation + maṇipuṣpaka jewel-flower) — Sughoṣa and Maṇipuṣpaka (objects of implied dadhmau/dadhmatuḥ). Rāmakantha 1.16a (in print, = our 1.15b): pauṇḍraṃ dadhmau mahāśaṅkhaṃ bhīmakarmā vṛkodaraḥ — see 1.15 wordMeaning. Sandhi: sahadevaḥ + ca → sahadevaśca (visarga → ś)."
