    "section": "Adhyāya 1 — Arjuna-viṣāda (Grief)",
    "devanagari": "योत्स्यमानानवेक्षेऽहं य एतेऽत्र समागताः। धार्तराष्ट्रस्य दुर्बुद्धेर्युद्धे प्रियचिकीर्षवः॥1.23॥",
    "iast": "yotsyamānānavekṣehṃ ya etetra samāgatāḥ dhārtarāṣṭrasya durbuddheryuddhe priyacikīrṣavḥ",
    "conceptIds": ["gita-visada"],
    "interpretiveNotes": [{"note": "Kashmir Series of Texts and Studies No. LXIV (1943): Bhagavadgītā with Sarvatobhadra of Rājānaka Rāmakantha, ed. Madhusudan Kaul Shastri; Sanskrit-only source, English rendering original to this project."}, {"note": "Mūla scan-verified (PDF p23, KSTS p13): yotsyamānān avekṣe 'haṃ ya ete 'tra samāgatāḥ ॥ २३ ॥ / dhārtarāṣṭrasya durbuddher yuddhe priya-cikīrṣavaḥ — our devanagari follows this print exactly."}, {"note": "Rāmakantha versification (PDF p23): the edition's 1.23 reads kair mayā saha yoddhavyam asmin raṇa-samudyame / yotsyamānān avekṣe 'haṃ ya ete 'tra samāgatāḥ — i.e. our 1.22b + our 1.23a. Our 1.23b (dhārtarāṣṭrasya durbuddher…) stands as its 1.24a. We keep vulgate versification for id-stability; sense identical."}, {"note": "Sarvatobhadra adhyāya-tātparya (PDF p26, KSTS p16): 'atha vyavasthitān dṛṣṭvā' ityādi upakramya sañjayoktāni eva arjuna-vākyāni sainya-nirūpaṇa-upakramāṇi 'ubhayoḥ senayor madhye' ityādīni subodhāni — this verse closes the three-verse inspection opening (1.21–1.23), self-evident, needing no word-gloss. Governing doctrine (PDF p25, KSTS p15): the all-doer Lord never loses kartṛtva, so kriyā-śakti never leaves the jīvas — hence even hostile tendencies, once beheld, can be purified (saṃskṛta) by jñāna into mokṣa-hetu rather than abandoned."}],
    "translation": "I would closely observe those about to fight, who have gathered here — desiring in battle to do what is dear to the evil-minded son of Dhṛtarāṣṭra.",
    "commentary": "Plain meaning, fully drawn out: Arjuna sharpens the survey commanded at 1.21 and extended at 1.22, and the verse divides into act of observing and moral character of the observed. Yotsyamānān avekṣe 'haṃ ya ete 'tra samāgatāḥ — 'I would observe those about to fight, who have gathered here': yotsyamānān (future middle participle of √yudh — about-to-fight, on the verge of fighting: nearer than 1.22's yoddhu-kāmān, who merely desire battle — these are already kindled toward it), avekṣe (1st singular ātmanepada of ava-√īkṣ — look down upon, oversee, observe with authority: ava- gives the gaze of command, after 1.22's nis- gave scrutiny; scrutiny matures into oversight), aham (I — the observer still steady), ye (who — relative opening the character-clause), ete (these — again deictic, present), atra (here — on Kurukṣetra, the dharma-field made history at 1.1), samāgatāḥ (gathered together — sam-ā-√gam: the same coming-together as 1.1's samāgame, now beheld from inside rather than asked about from outside). Dhārtarāṣṭrasya durbuddher yuddhe priya-cikīrṣavaḥ — 'desiring in battle to do what is dear to Duryodhana of evil mind': dhārtarāṣṭrasya (of Dhṛtarāṣṭra's son — singular, Duryodhana named by blind-fatherhood, as at 1.19's dhārtarāṣṭrāṇām plural), durbuddheḥ (of evil mind — dur + buddhi: judgement gone awry, intellect unlit by ātman; the verse's only condemnation, placed centrally), yuddhe (in battle — locative of occasion), priya-cikīrṣavaḥ (desiring to do the dear — priya + cikīrṣā desiderative of √kṛ: whose wish is to please, mercenaries of affection). The moral judgement is already forming, yet still judicial: the foe fights not for dharma-mother-father (1.5's maternal hinge) but to gratify folly — mad-arthe (1.9) now given its proper name. Sarvatobhadra meaning, fully drawn out: subodhāni — and the plainness judges. Rāmakantha lets the seeker's clarity stand as doctrine: viveka first marks (pariccheda) before it burns. The yotsyamānān are the seeker's own vṛttis on the verge of discharge — icchās already serving ahaṃkāra-as-durbuddhi — and avekṣā (oversight) is their lawful preface to offering: the jñānāgni of 4.19+ will receive exactly these priya-seeking tendencies, for only the beheld can be purified (saṃskṛta) by jñāna into mokṣa-hetu (PDF p25). Durbuddhi inwardly is buddhi unyoked from the Self — 1.14's yukte inverted — and priya-cikīrṣā is kriyā-śakti conscripted by faction: the same śakti that, recognised, sounds as proclamation (1.19's ghoṣa) here labours unrecognised as flattery of delusion. That is why the verse honours inspection without yet censuring action: marking bondage precisely is the first step to burning it, and the middle (madhye, 1.21) is held open just long enough for this marking — before Kṛṣṇa's paśya (1.25, sphuṭārtha) turns marked tendencies into beheld kin, and kinship detonates viṣāda, the pravṛtti-hetu of grace.",
    "keyPoints": ["yotsyamānān: nearer than yoddhu-kāmān — verge of discharge", "avekṣe after nirīkṣe: scrutiny matures into oversight", "ye ete atra samāgatāḥ: asked-about assembly now beheld from inside", "dhārtarāṣṭrasya singular: folly given a proper name", "durbuddheḥ: intellect unlit by ātman, verse's sole condemnation", "priya-cikīrṣavaḥ: mercenaries of affection, mad-arthe renamed", "still judicial: judgement forming, not yet lamenting", "marking before burning: pariccheda precedes jñānāgni", "kriyā-śakti conscripted: proclamation inverted as flattery", "middle held open for marking before paśya detonates grief"],
    "wordMeaning": "yotsyamānān (accusative plural masculine, future middle participle of √yudh) — about to fight; avekṣe (1st singular present ātmanepada of ava-√īkṣ) — I observe, oversee; aham (nominative singular of asmad) — I; ye (nominative plural masculine relative) — who; ete (nominative plural masculine demonstrative) — these; atra (adverb) — here; samāgatāḥ (nominative plural masculine, past participle of sam-ā-√gam) — gathered together; dhārtarāṣṭrasya (genitive singular masculine, dhṛtarāṣṭra + aṇ) — of Dhṛtarāṣṭra's son, Duryodhana; durbuddheḥ (genitive singular feminine-masculine, dur + buddhi) — of evil mind; yuddhe (locative singular neuter) — in battle; priya-cikīrṣavaḥ (nominative plural masculine, priya + cikīrṣu desiderative agent of √kṛ) — desiring to do what is dear, wishing to please. Rāmakantha 1.23a (in print, = our 1.22b): kair mayā saha yoddhavyam asmin raṇa-samudyame — see 1.22 wordMeaning. Sandhi: yotsyamānān + avekṣe → yotsyamānānavekṣe; avekṣe + aham → avekṣe 'ham (e + a → e with avagraha); ye + ete → ya ete (e + e → a with avagraha in print ye 'te); ete + atra → ete 'tra; durbuddheḥ + yuddhe → durbuddheryuddhe (visarga → r)."
